+    "text": "RE-24 answer (SIM, sim6 v2.13-dev amendment J, bit-check v1.1 ALL PASS, liq|G73|v351|h210, N=500 per base, jam bound ASSUMED from Electronics S6/SIL v2.10: 1.5 deg + 40 ms x model rate, 50 ms): a landing-burn jam that the monitor does not trip on before touchdown lost no landing in this set: 31 runs per base and servo, all jams in the last 0.25-1.19 s before the twin's touchdown (onset u >= 0.742 of the landing burn; every earlier jam was detected), 0 lost / 0 gained against the no-jam twin (one-sided 95 % UB 9.2 % per arm; 60 distinct runs over the four arms) (landed with the jam / no-jam twin: A1_none servo A 30/31 / 30, servo B 29/31 / 29; A1J_pc 31/31 / 31, 30/31 / 30). Nominal flights (mon2): false GIMBAL_FAULT aborts 0/1000 (UB95 0.30 %); nominal metric max 0.18-0.19 deg vs 1.5 deg with the flexible mount and encoder quantisation modelled; encoder noise, vibration and supply sag not modelled. Detected jams are another matter: a landing-burn jam lands in 0.136-0.150 of runs if flown on and 0.084-0.088 with the monitor's abort (detected A1_none servo A: continue 42/469, abort 13/469); a coast or ascent jam is lost either way (P 0.000-0.002). Landed fraction by time-to-go at the jam: [0, 1) s 59/84, [1, 2) s 12/210, >= 2 s about 0. So the abort costs landings in the last ~1 s; whether to inhibit the GIMBAL_FAULT abort near touchdown was put to the lead as a safety decision (a jammed engine at up to ~20 deg may leave the range safety envelope); it is DECIDED: NOT inhibited (227 (7), below). Not modelled: stall current sag on the shared supply, encoder faults, a roll recovery mode. LEAD SAFETY DECISION (227 (7)): RE-24, lead safety decision: the GIMBAL_FAULT abort is NOT inhibited near touchdown; a detected landing-burn jam aborts as designed. Why: continuing gains landing P 0.136-0.150 against 0.084-0.088 only inside a failure whose rate is unknown; a thrust cut gives a more predictable impact point than a lit engine held at a jammed angle; and an inhibit adds a mode to verify. Revisit if B-TVC-1 gives a jam rate. The undetected-jam result carries the RV6-06 scope (31 runs per base and servo, all late jams, 0 lost / 0 gained, one-sided UB95 9.2 % per arm); 0/1000 false aborts (UB95 0.30 %; encoder noise, vibration and supply sag not modelled).",
